---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { base } from '../../../utils/base.js';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import ChoroplethFigure from '../../../components/ChoroplethFigure.astro';
import WorldbuildingInteractiveMap from '../../../components/WorldbuildingInteractiveMap.astro';
import terrainHero from '../../../assets/images/worldbuilding-terrain.png';
import biomeMap from '../../../assets/images/worldbuilding-biomes.png';
import hydrologyMap from '../../../assets/images/worldbuilding-hydrology.png';
import precipMap from '../../../assets/images/worldbuilding-precip.png';
import tempMap from '../../../assets/images/worldbuilding-temp.png';
import suitabilityMap from '../../../assets/images/worldbuilding-suitability.png';
---
<BaseLayout title="World-building GDR - Nico">
  <div class="page-container">
    <div class="case-study">
      <header class="case-hero">
        <p class="eyebrow">Case Study — World-building</p>
        <h1>Building the Ground Beneath a Solarpunk World</h1>
        <p class="intro">
          When I left my public-sector GIS job, I used the time to explore
          directions the role never allowed — including spatializing a
          fictional setting I'd been building for a homebrew Solarpunk
          campaign (an original adaptation of the Open D6 system). Rather
          than draw a map by hand, I treated it as a real modeling problem:
          terrain, climate, hydrology, biomes, and settlement suitability,
          generated as a proper GIS pipeline instead of a static
          illustration.
        </p>
      </header>
    </div>

    <figure class="hero-split">
      <Image
        src={suitabilityMap}
        alt="Círculo settlement suitability composite with 17 placed sites"
        widths={[400, 800, 1200]}
        sizes="(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 60vw"
        format="webp"
        quality={80}
      />
      <div class="hero-text">
        <h2>Latency: Settlement Suitability</h2>
        <p>17 <em>Círculo</em> sites, placed by a five-criteria suitability model — slope, water access, solar exposure, agriculture, and biome.</p>
      </div>
    </figure>
    <figcaption class="map-source">130 × 160 km domain · 17 <em>Círculo</em> sites (4 large · 5 medium · 8 small)</figcaption>

    <div class="case-study">
      <section class="context">
        <p class="intro">
          <em>Latency</em> is the setting for a Solarpunk campaign: a society
          organized into autonomous, decentralized settlements called
          <em>Círculos</em>, built around cooperation and sustainability
          rather than hierarchy or scarcity. I wanted the physical
          geography behind that setting — mountains, rainfall, rivers,
          biomes, where people could plausibly live — to be internally
          consistent, not just visually plausible. This is the second
          time I've built this world with GIS methods; the first pass
          had no version control and cut corners I wanted to fix. Unlike
          the Torino case study, there's no sensitive real-world data
          here, which meant I could push the pipeline further technically
          and publish it without anonymization.
        </p>
        <p class="callout">
          <strong>A note on process:</strong> this pipeline was built with
          AI-assisted coding (Claude Cowork) rather than written
          line-by-line, which let me reach a level of complexity — six
          linked stages, each with its own model and validation step —
          well beyond what I could have scripted solo in the same
          timeframe. My role was to direct the pipeline, make the
          methodological calls, and validate outputs, mostly by checking
          results spatially against my own GIS and fieldwork background.
          That background covers terrain reading, hydrology, and
          cartographic judgment well; it doesn't cover the
          theoretical/mathematical foundations of some of the models
          involved, particularly in the climate and snow stages — those I
          can validate by result, not independently derive or fully
          audit.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="methodology">

        <article class="step">
          <h2>1. Terrain</h2>
          <p>
            The island sits at roughly 44° south latitude — deliberately
            modeled on the scale and topography of New Zealand's South
            Island — inside a custom 130 × 160 km domain. The mountainous
            spine and coastal plateaus were hand-digitized in QGIS as a
            "skeleton" of ridgelines and flat zones, then built out with
            Simplex noise and a droplet-based hydraulic erosion model,
            domain-warped to avoid the unnaturally regular coastlines
            noise alone tends to produce. Land ends up at 45.3% of the
            130 × 160 km domain — an island, not a continent fragment.
          </p>
          <p>
            The second version had a problem I could see before I could
            name it: comparing it to real hillshade and topographic
            references, the terrain read as organic and curvy in a way no
            real relief does — closer to coral than rock. I pushed back
            and asked for a third pass rather than accept it. The
            underlying cause turned out to be a geometric defect in how
            the terrain's texture was being warped, affecting close to a
            third of the map; the fix — found by adjustment, not derived
            outright — cut that down to a negligible fraction, checked
            across several regions before moving forward.
          </p>
          <div class="analysis-map-wrap">
            <Image
              src={terrainHero}
              alt="Hillshaded, hypsometrically tinted elevation model of Latency"
              widths={[400, 800, 1200]}
              sizes="(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 70vw"
              format="webp"
              quality={80}
            />
          </div>
          <figcaption class="map-source">custom Lambert Conformal Conic · land 45.3% of domain</figcaption>
        </article>

        <article class="step">
          <h2>2. Climate</h2>
          <p>
            Monthly temperature and precipitation come from a linear
            orographic model (Smith & Barstad, 2004): prevailing westerly
            winds hit the island's mountain spine and drop their moisture
            on the windward slopes, leaving a drier band in the rain
            shadow to the east — the same pattern that shapes the
            Southern Alps, which I used throughout as a real-world
            reference to check that the resulting temperature and
            rainfall patterns were physically reasonable. That contrast —
            wet, temperate coasts on one side; drier, more continental
            conditions on the other — is the backbone of where the
            setting's biomes and, later, its architecture diverge.
          </p>
          <div class="choropleth-grid">
            <ChoroplethFigure
              img={precipMap}
              alt="Annual precipitation total, Latency"
              title="Annual precipitation (mm)"
              legend={[
                { color: '#cfe8e6', label: '< 470 mm' },
                { color: '#8fc3c2', label: '470–1,718 mm' },
                { color: '#4f9396', label: '1,718–3,958 mm' },
                { color: '#215f66', label: '> 3,958 mm' },
              ]}
            />
            <ChoroplethFigure
              img={tempMap}
              alt="Annual mean temperature, Latency"
              title="Annual mean temperature (°C)"
              legend={[
                { color: '#9ab8d6', label: '< 3.9 °C' },
                { color: '#d9c49a', label: '3.9–8.7 °C' },
                { color: '#d98f5a', label: '8.7–10.9 °C' },
                { color: '#b84a3a', label: '> 10.9 °C' },
              ]}
            />
          </div>
          <figcaption class="map-source">Smith & Barstad (2004) orographic model · quantile class breaks</figcaption>
        </article>

        <article class="step">
          <h2>3. Snow and permanent ice</h2>
          <p>
            The interior's highest peaks hold permanent snow and ice,
            mapped from a positive-degree-day mass-balance model
            (monthly accumulation vs. melt) rather than a fixed elevation
            cutoff — the boundary shifts with slope and aspect, not just
            altitude.
          </p>
          <p class="callout">
            <strong>Still under review:</strong> this is the stage I
            trust the least so far and am actively still studying — I'd
            rather hold back specific figures (like exact snowline
            elevation) until I can validate them with more confidence
            myself.
          </p>
          <!-- TODO: permanent snow / ELA map -->
        </article>

        <article class="step">
          <h2>4. Hydrology</h2>
          <p>
            Flow direction and stream extraction use the Priority-Flood +
            Epsilon algorithm (Barnes, Lehman & Mulla, 2014), which fills
            depressions and derives drainage in a single pass. Rivers run
            off the mountain spine toward both coasts, feeding lakes in
            the larger valleys — the water network that determines where
            a Círculo can realistically draw water, farm, or trade by
            boat. Two details came from years of fieldwork surveying real
            watercourses: the first draft of the stream network looked
            mechanically straight in a way no real channel does, so I
            asked for it to be redrawn with more organic curves; and the
            first version had no seasonal variation, so I asked for flow
            to shift with monthly rainfall and temperature — dry-season
            creeks, wet-season floods — the way I've seen it behave in
            the field.
          </p>
          <p>
            One cross-check: the discharge ratio between windward and
            leeward basins came out at 5.9:1, close to the 5.6:1
            precipitation ratio the climate stage had already validated
            independently — the kind of internal consistency check that
            doesn't prove the model right, but is a real signal it isn't
            badly wrong.
          </p>
          <div class="analysis-map-wrap">
            <Image
              src={hydrologyMap}
              alt="Drainage network and lakes derived from the DEM, major channels only"
              widths={[400, 800, 1200]}
              sizes="(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 70vw"
              format="webp"
              quality={82}
            />
          </div>
          <figcaption class="map-source">Priority-Flood + Epsilon flow routing, thresholded to major channels for legibility at this scale</figcaption>
        </article>

        <article class="step">
          <h2>5. Biomes</h2>
          <p>
            Land cover was classified with the Holdridge Life Zone
            system, which places each cell by temperature and rainfall
            rather than by hand — producing the dry rain-shadow scrub of
            the interior, temperate forest on the wet coasts, and alpine
            zones near the permanent snow line. This map isn't just
            decorative: it's what the setting bible's regional
            architecture styles are built on directly — rammed-earth,
            half-buried construction in the cold mountain valleys;
            raised, woven structures in the dense temperate forest; open,
            ventilated coastal building in the drier plains — each style
            tied to a specific biome this stage produced, not chosen
            freehand.
          </p>
          <p class="callout">
            <strong>Methodological call:</strong> Holdridge's standard
            aridity thresholds are calibrated for hot deserts, and
            applied unmodified here they never registered anything as
            dry, no matter how little a place actually rained — a cool,
            temperate island just doesn't generate enough
            evapotranspiration to trip a desert-calibrated scale.
            Rescaling that axis to the island's own climate range,
            instead of forcing Holdridge's absolute categories, was a
            deliberate decision once the data made the mismatch obvious.
          </p>
          <ChoroplethFigure
            img={biomeMap}
            alt="Biome classification of Latency, Holdridge Life Zone system"
            title="Biomes (Holdridge Life Zone system)"
            legend={[
              { color: '#e0a83f', label: 'Lowland Steppe / Grassland — 2,962 km²' },
              { color: '#7a9c4a', label: 'Woodland / Shrubland — 2,835 km²' },
              { color: '#2e7d46', label: 'Temperate Forest — 690 km²' },
              { color: '#1f6f54', label: 'Subalpine Wet Forest — 942 km²' },
              { color: '#4f8a5c', label: 'Subalpine Woodland — 167 km²' },
              { color: '#b8622e', label: 'Subalpine Dry Scrub — 335 km²' },
              { color: '#a89bb0', label: 'Alpine Tundra — 617 km²' },
              { color: '#8f8579', label: 'Alpine Fellfield — 396 km²' },
              { color: '#f5f7f8', label: 'Permanent Snow & Ice — 960 km²' },
              { color: '#bcdcee', label: 'Ocean — 10,919 km²' },
            ]}
          />
        </article>

        <article class="step">
          <h2>6. Settlement suitability</h2>
          <p>
            The final stage scores the land for settlement viability —
            slope, water proximity, solar exposure (an FAO-56 clear-sky
            radiation model, computed from scratch since GRASS's
            <code>r.sun</code> wasn't available), agricultural potential,
            biome — for two culturally distinct readings of the land: the
            structured, permanent Círculos get discrete candidate sites;
            the Povo Livre, groups descended from exiles who never
            settled into fixed communities, get a continuous suitability
            surface instead of point sites — a deliberate difference in
            output, not a simplification, since a nomadic culture doesn't
            have "candidate settlements" in the same sense. A separate
            exclusion layer keeps candidate sites clear of the remote territories
            associated with the Povo Silencioso — respecting, spatially,
            a piece of the setting's own lore about a reclusive
            non-human people. One methodological note worth naming:
            slope was computed at native 30 m resolution then
            block-max downsampled (not averaged) to the coarser working
            grid, since a simple average understated the land's real
            slope by close to half — the wrong direction of error for a
            hazard criterion.
          </p>
          <p>
            All 17 Círculos placements (four large, five medium, eight
            small) came from this stage. Distance between candidates was computed with Dijkstra's
            algorithm over Tobler's hiking-function cost surface
            (accounting for terrain difficulty, not just straight-line
            distance) rather than simple radius exclusion, after an early
            version placed unrealistic clusters. Manually reviewing the
            output caught real problems the pipeline's own checks missed
            — settlements placed inside lakes, two settlements placed too
            close together across categories that weren't being
            cross-checked — a good reminder that visual review and
            automated testing catch different kinds of mistakes.
          </p>
          <p class="map-source">Full composite shown at the top of the page — slope, water proximity, solar exposure, agriculture, biome, weighted, markers sized by settlement tier (large/medium/small).</p>

          <WorldbuildingInteractiveMap />
          <!-- TODO: 3D drape (MapLibre raster-dem terrain), sequenced after
               this stage per the Tappa 0 decision — deliberately not part of
               this interactive map, see Livello 3 outline. -->
        </article>

      </section>

      <section class="result">
        <h2>Result</h2>
        <p>
17 Círculo settlement sites, on a 130 × 160 km island where land
          covers 45.3% of the domain and biomes range from roughly 2,960
          km² of lowland steppe in the rain shadow to under 1,000 km² of
          permanent snow and ice on the interior peaks — consistent with
          the terrain, climate, water access, and biome built up over the
          previous five stages. The Povo Livre's relationship to the same
          land is a separate suitability surface, not a set of points —
          fitting for a culture defined by not settling. A physically
          grounded map for a setting that started as worldbuilding notes
          and TTRPG lore, now with a geography that could, in principle,
          be surveyed. The
          pipeline stops here for portfolio purposes — further layers
          (regional detail, per-settlement zoom) are planned, but as
          depth for the campaign itself rather than additional case study
          material.
        </p>
      </section>

      <a class="cta-button" href={`${base}/en/case-study-worldbuilding/technical/`}>
        Read the technical deep dive →
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-container {
    padding-left: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 2cm);
    padding-top: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 2cm);
    padding-bottom: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 2cm);
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .page-container {
      padding-left: 1.5rem;
      padding-top: 1.5rem;
      padding-bottom: 1.5rem;
    }
  }
  .case-study {
    max-width: 42rem;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-brass);
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
  }
  .case-hero h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 2rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-paper);
    line-height: 1.25;
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
  }
  .intro, .step p:not(.callout), .result p:not(.callout) {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted-2);
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }
  .context {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }
  .step {
    margin: 2.5rem 0;
    padding-top: 2rem;
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(198, 146, 20, 0.12);
  }
  .step h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.3rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-paper);
    margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
  }
  p.callout {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--color-paper);
    background: var(--color-ink-light);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--color-brass);
    padding: 0.85rem 1.1rem;
    border-radius: 4px;
    margin: 1rem 0;
  }
  .result {
    margin: 3rem 0 2.5rem;
    padding-top: 2rem;
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(198, 146, 20, 0.12);
  }
  .result h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.3rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-brass);
    margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
  }
  .cta-button {
    display: inline-block;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    background: var(--color-brass);
    padding: 0.6rem 1.2rem;
    border-radius: 4px;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .hero-split {
    display: flex;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    align-items: flex-start;
    margin: 2rem 0 0;
    max-width: 58rem;
    width: 58rem;
  }
  .hero-split img {
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    min-width: 0;
    border-radius: 8px;
    display: block;
  }
  .hero-text {
    flex: 0 0 15rem;
    background: var(--color-paper);
    color: var(--color-ink);
    padding: 1.25rem;
    border-radius: 8px;
  }
  .hero-text h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 0 0 0.65rem;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }
  .hero-text p {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .map-source {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }
  .analysis-map-wrap {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }
  .analysis-map-wrap img {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: 8px;
    display: block;
  }
  .choropleth-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: 1.25rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
    width: 58rem;
    max-width: 100%;
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .choropleth-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .hero-split {
      width: 100%;
    }
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .hero-split {
      flex-direction: column;
    }
    .hero-text {
      flex: 0 0 auto;
    }
  }
</style>
